    "q-72140": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72140",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252915/assets/c72f0a6f9bfb83be34cf911d2a2a18b04a40f92f4d1f6c065b9d6f03f56301b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2(x-1)^{3}+1\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1\n$$\n\nThe function $x=0$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14252915/assets/1dcfc91a9392998f6de0e4ede2e67d9487f71bb521adad024c3c6891b56d0163.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=0$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y-1}{2}}+1\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-72140-a",
